      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2898.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 685,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is famous for being utterly ordinary yet profoundly bizarre in its behavior compared to other liquids.\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rivers), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure (it has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side), water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances. This is why things like salt, sugar, and vitamins dissolve easily in it. It’s also how water transports nutrients through our bodies and minerals through the earth.\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a glass. In nature, this is crucial: because ice floats on top of lakes and oceans, it creates an insulating layer that allows marine life to survive the winter underneath.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth’s surface** is covered in water, mostly by the oceans. \n* **Scarcity:** Despite the Earth being covered in water, roughly **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans, which humans cannot drink. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and ponds.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiology cannot exist without water. \n* **The Medium of Life:** The first living organisms evolved in Earth's oceans, and life still depends on aqueous (water-based) environments to function.\n* **Human Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water. It regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and carries oxygen through our blood. Without water, a human can only survive for about 3 days.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nWater is the ultimate connector. It shapes the geography of our planet, drives our weather and climate, makes agriculture and industry possible, and flows through the veins of every living thing."
